WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.680
Memory corruption means code accesses, modifies, or interprets memory outside the program's intended rules.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:17.380
In C, those rules include which object owns the storage, how large the object is, how long it remains alive, which type is being used to access it, and which code is responsible for cleanup.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.760
When one of those assumptions is wrong, the program may keep running while its internal state is no longer trustworthy.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.460
C gives developers direct memory control.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:33.880
That control is useful for systems software, embedded work, runtime libraries, parsers, drivers, and performance-sensitive components.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.660
It also means the language does not automatically enforce every size, lifetime, ownership, or type rule at runtime.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.680
The code has to carry and check those facts deliberately.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:53.660
The impact of memory corruption can range from a crash to incorrect results, corrupted records, lost state, accidental data exposure, or security defects.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.480
The safest way to frame the issue is as an engineering reliability problem with security consequences.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:05.780
If code reads or writes the wrong memory, later decisions may be based on data that no longer represents reality.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.960
Many memory corruption problems begin as ordinary mistakes rather than dramatic failures.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.500
A loop runs one element too far.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.200
A pointer outlives the object it referenced.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.100
A string lacks a terminator.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.760
A buffer grows but its capacity field is not updated.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.340
A cleanup path frees something twice or forgets who owns it.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.400
These are reviewable defects.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:33.220
Defensive C programming means treating memory assumptions as explicit design decisions.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:41.200
Size, lifetime, ownership, type, valid range, and cleanup behavior should be visible in interfaces and code paths.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:50.140
This course focuses on prevention, detection, maintainability, and responsible engineering habits that make memory defects easier to find before release.
